\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Binary local spinor norms and the Spin index}

\p{Let #{K} be a nonarchimedean local field of characteristic different
from two, as in \ref{fcap-0044}, and let #{Q} be a nondegenerate form on
a finite-dimensional #{K}-space. Use the reverse-norm convention of
\ref{fcap-002D}: #{\nu_Q^O} is the spinor norm on #{O(Q)}, and
#{\nu_Q} is its restriction to #{SO(Q)}. Write

##{\begin{gathered}
S=K^{\times}/(K^{\times})^2,\\
I_Q=[SO(Q):\rho(\Spin(Q))].
\end{gathered}}

The kernel theorem \ref{fcap-0023} gives
#{I_Q=|\operatorname{im}\nu_Q|}. Thus the index of the Spin image in
#{SO(Q)} measures the size of the spinor-norm image, not its index in #{S}.}

\p{For the anisotropic form #{Q_+=\langle1,1\rangle} over
#{\mathbb Q_3} from \ref{fcap-0048}, both spinor-norm images are the
square classes of norms from #{\mathbb Q_3(\sqrt{-1})}. They form the
same index-two subgroup of #{S}. Since #{|S|=4}, the Spin index is
#{I_{Q_+}=2}. The square-class count is
\citet{VI.2, Theorem 2.22, p. 162}{lam2005quadratic}; the norm-image
calculation follows from the general binary formulas below.}

\p{Suppose #{Q=\langle a,b\rangle} is anisotropic, with
#{a,b\in K^\times}, and put #{d=-b/a}. Then #{d} is nonsquare and
#{E=K(\sqrt d)} is a quadratic field. Its field norm is

##{N_{E/K}(x+y\sqrt d)=x^2-dy^2.}

The \newvocab{norm subgroup} #{N=N_{E/K}(E^\times)} contains
#{(K^\times)^2}, since #{N_{E/K}(c)=c^2} for #{c\in K^\times}.
Let #{H=N/(K^\times)^2\subseteq S}. Then

##{\begin{gathered}
\operatorname{im}\nu_Q=H,\\
\operatorname{im}\nu_Q^O=H\cup[a]H,\\
[S:H]=2,\\
I_Q=|H|=|S|/2.
\end{gathered}}

In particular, #{\nu_Q^O} is surjective exactly when #{a\notin N}.
If #{a\in N}, both images are #{H}. If #{Q} is split binary instead,
both images are #{S} and #{I_Q=|S|}.}

\p{The lower-dimensional boundary, with #{0} denoting the form on the
zero space, is

##{\begin{array}{c|c|c}
Q&\operatorname{im}\nu_Q&\operatorname{im}\nu_Q^O\\ \hline
0&\{1\}&\{1\}\\
\langle a\rangle&\{1\}&\langle[a]\rangle
\end{array}}

In both rows #{SO(Q)} is trivial and #{I_Q=1}. In rank one,
#{O(Q)=\{1,-1\}}, and #{-1} is reflection in the basis vector of
value #{a}; its norm is #{[a]}. These statements agree with
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.spinorNorm_eq_one_of_finrank_le_one}
and
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.orthogonalSpinorNorm_range_eq_zpowers_of_finrank_eq_one}.}

\subtree{
\taxon{Proof}
\p{For the anisotropic binary form, the nonzero represented values are
exactly #{aN}, because #{Q(x,y)=a(x^2-dy^2)}. Every isometry is a
product of reflections by \ref{fcap-000W}, and determinant parity
counts the factors by \ref{fcap-001A}. Its spinor norm is the square class
of the product of their quadratic values \ref{fcap-002D}. Even products
therefore have class in #{H}. Conversely, choose vectors with values
#{a} and #{an} for any #{n\in N}. Their reflection product has class
#{[a^2n]=[n]}, so every class of #{H} occurs on #{SO(Q)}. Multiplying
by the reflection of value #{a} gives the odd coset #{[a]H}, proving
the formula on #{O(Q)}. For a split binary form, the hyperbolic
coordinate form #{xy} represents every nonzero scalar; reflections
of values #{1} and #{c} give any prescribed class #{[c]}.}

\p{To obtain the index of #{N}, use the local \newvocab{Hilbert symbol}
#{(u,v)_K}: it is #{1} when the
\vocabk{quaternion algebra}{fgap-0006} #{(u,v\mid K)} is a matrix
algebra, and #{-1} otherwise. This is a symmetric bilinear pairing
on #{S}, nondegenerate by
\citet{VI.2, discussion on p. 159 and Theorem 2.16, p. 160}{lam2005quadratic}.
The quaternion norm criterion
\citet{III.2, Theorem 2.7(1), (8) and proof, pp. 58--59}{lam2005quadratic}
says that #{(n,d)_K=1} exactly when #{n\in N}. Since #{[d]\ne1},
nondegeneracy makes #{[n]\mapsto(n,d)_K} onto #{\{1,-1\}}.
Its kernel is #{H}, so #{[S:H]=2}. The formula for #{I_Q} follows
from \ref{fcap-0023}. For #{Q_+}, the first coefficient is #{1\in N},
so the two norm images coincide, as claimed.}
}

\p{The two binary image formulas are
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.range_spinorNorm_of_equivalent_binary}
and
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.range_orthogonalSpinorNorm_of_equivalent_binary}.
They use the parameter #{-ab=a^2d}, which gives the same quadratic
extension and norm subgroup. The local index and orthogonal
surjectivity criterion are
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.index_range_spinorNorm_of_finrank_eq_two_of_anisotropic}
and
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.orthogonalSpinorNorm_surjective_iff_not_mem_quadraticNormSubgroup_of_anisotropic_binary}.
The image formulas require finite dimension, nondegeneracy and invertible
two over a field; the index-two conclusion additionally uses the stated
local-field and anisotropy hypotheses.}
